WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Yes, thank you. So, I am an assistant professor at Delft University

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.550
of Technology. My name is Martijn Leijten.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.630
And I do not have any direct ties to either Bulgaria or Turkey,

4
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.430
except that one of my PhD students is Turkish.

5
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.840
I am going to tell you a slightly more abstract and

6
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.310
academic story, but I'll try to make it as illustrative as possible.

7
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.840
And I want to talk about what technology exchange communities

8
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.720
look like a bit, because that was the topic of this session,

9
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:41.920
right? They said actually "technology transfer,"

10
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.070
but I think that sounds a bit one-directional.

11
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.040
So I'd like to talk about technology exchange or knowledge exchange

12
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:49.940
about technology.

13
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.560
There are basically two ways in which a community that exchanges

14
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.720
knowledge about technology can look, and these are two extremes

15
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.830
of course, but there are all kinds of shapes in between.

16
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.720
And this is sort of a disassortative or an assortative network of actors,

17
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.270
parties, companies,

18
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.310
the scientific community,

19
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.040
government, etc. If we look at them, the difference between them

20
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.080
is the disassortative. What you see is basically A and B.

21
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.840
A is the disassortative one, B is the assortative one, and then C

22
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.560
and D are a picture of how they develop over time.

23
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.070
New parties enter the community and make it expand.

24
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.440
The disassortative network is one where you see grouped parties

25
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.790
involved in this exchange process.

26
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.800
And one of the main characteristics of them is that they are very

27
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.360
different. So there's a large variety of parties.

28
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.612
Some may be companies around one specific technology,

29
00:01:58.612 --> 00:02:03.052
and maybe about a completely different technology. That could

30
00:02:03.052 --> 00:02:08.732
be users, etc. For example, in the development of a new railway

31
00:02:08.732 --> 00:02:11.242
traffic management system in Europe, the ERTMS,

32
00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:15.562
one of the components of the technology is communication technology,

33
00:02:16.012 --> 00:02:18.732
which is something completely different from what train

34
00:02:18.732 --> 00:02:20.572
manufacturers do, for example.

35
00:02:20.572 --> 00:02:21.922
But they're all in a network.

36
00:02:22.852 --> 00:02:26.132
Now, the problem with this is, as you can also see,

37
00:02:26.132 --> 00:02:30.372
there are direct connections with all kinds

38
00:02:30.372 --> 00:02:34.842
of parties that group around one of those actors,

39
00:02:35.292 --> 00:02:38.362
one-on-one relationships typically.

40
00:02:38.932 --> 00:02:43.682
But then there are only few relationships between the clusters.

41
00:02:44.532 --> 00:02:46.682
And that makes the network quite vulnerable.

42
00:02:47.212 --> 00:02:51.122
So it could be that, for example, when communication technology

43
00:02:51.532 --> 00:02:53.732
goes in a completely different direction, you know,

44
00:02:53.732 --> 00:02:56.772
with the train traffic management example, that that part of the

45
00:02:56.772 --> 00:03:00.492
network disappears. And then, basically, the whole exchange

46
00:03:00.492 --> 00:03:04.882
community can collapse. So that could be problematic.

47
00:03:05.412 --> 00:03:08.852
This type of network is quite fragile because of the lack of

48
00:03:08.852 --> 00:03:12.562
redundancy. There are very few ties between those clusters.

49
00:03:13.012 --> 00:03:17.532
So the extreme alternative is an assortative network where the

50
00:03:17.532 --> 00:03:19.732
parties are much more alike.

51
00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:23.492
So that's how they get connected.

52
00:03:23.492 --> 00:03:26.812
And so they clump up, as you can see there.

53
00:03:26.812 --> 00:03:31.492
The way the network develops is that there are a few

54
00:03:31.492 --> 00:03:36.052
central parties that really get close connection with

55
00:03:36.052 --> 00:03:40.372
each other. And they are typically the ones that define what

56
00:03:40.372 --> 00:03:43.172
direction the technology development is going,

57
00:03:43.172 --> 00:03:45.652
because they are clearly the most important ones in the

58
00:03:45.652 --> 00:03:50.772
network. But with them, they drag a very long tail of parties that

59
00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:54.962
have some interest, but are not really the core of the network.

60
00:03:55.572 --> 00:04:01.292
Now, this kind of network is relatively fragile

61
00:04:01.292 --> 00:04:04.734
because it has very unclear structures, relatively complex,

62
00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:08.934
yeah. So nobody really knows how all the relationships are

63
00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:11.924
organized, and they are very erratic.

64
00:04:12.574 --> 00:04:18.774
But it is quite a strong network because if one part just

65
00:04:18.774 --> 00:04:21.294
disappears, it will still be there.

66
00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:23.294
And it's a bit like the internet, for example.

67
00:04:23.294 --> 00:04:25.614
If it has one connection that fails, you know,

68
00:04:25.614 --> 00:04:28.484
the data will just go through another connection.

69
00:04:29.574 --> 00:04:34.454
So these are the two extremes for the configuration of

70
00:04:34.454 --> 00:04:38.214
a network where technology transfer takes place.

71
00:04:38.214 --> 00:04:43.884
So that is one aspect. I think a solid network would combine

72
00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:46.254
the best of the two worlds.

73
00:04:46.254 --> 00:04:50.694
So maybe a hybrid model would be the way out, where there is

74
00:04:50.694 --> 00:04:54.734
still a large diversity of parties involved, because typically we

75
00:04:54.734 --> 00:04:58.054
see that in these communities, the more diverse they are,

76
00:04:58.054 --> 00:05:00.174
the more new ideas develop.

77
00:05:00.174 --> 00:05:03.974
You know, if everyone thinks alike, you still always get the same

78
00:05:03.974 --> 00:05:09.134
solutions. So this diversity is important, but then maybe

79
00:05:09.134 --> 00:05:14.494
the fragility of this sort of network could be solved

80
00:05:14.494 --> 00:05:17.204
with more redundant connections.

81
00:05:17.774 --> 00:05:22.374
So whereas in this model, we see that there are only one-on-one

82
00:05:22.374 --> 00:05:26.124
connections within the cluster, here you see that everyone basically

83
00:05:27.454 --> 00:05:30.854
gets in connection with each other, a bit like a plate of

84
00:05:30.854 --> 00:05:37.574
spaghetti. Now, the other thing that I think is relevant is

85
00:05:37.574 --> 00:05:40.494
who controls what happens in the network.

86
00:05:40.494 --> 00:05:43.284
So what are the technologies that will become dominant?

87
00:05:43.974 --> 00:05:47.454
What is the knowledge that will be exchanged between the

88
00:05:47.454 --> 00:05:52.214
involved parties? And there are again roughly three different

89
00:05:52.214 --> 00:05:55.494
flavors. And again, these are extremes, of course.

90
00:05:55.494 --> 00:05:58.574
There's a centralized, a decentralized, and a distributed network.

91
00:05:58.574 --> 00:06:00.284
So there are pictures of them here.

92
00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:04.975
And to explain how this works in the exchange network or

93
00:06:04.975 --> 00:06:09.295
community, I would like to illustrate this with a completely

94
00:06:09.295 --> 00:06:14.615
different example, which is the development that has taken

95
00:06:14.615 --> 00:06:17.645
place in our consumption of video entertainment.

96
00:06:19.215 --> 00:06:23.975
Because we used to have a very centralized video entertainment

97
00:06:23.975 --> 00:06:29.365
system, meaning previously, maybe 30, 40 years ago,

98
00:06:29.935 --> 00:06:31.165
you would just watch television.

99
00:06:32.055 --> 00:06:34.445
So it was a very linear model.

100
00:06:34.855 --> 00:06:38.135
And the ones who decided what was on the television were the

101
00:06:38.135 --> 00:06:39.245
broadcasting corporations.

102
00:06:40.175 --> 00:06:43.135
So you switched on the television, and you would just watch what was

103
00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:45.325
on there at the time that it was available.

104
00:06:45.975 --> 00:06:49.375
Very long time ago, there was only one channel.

105
00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:52.685
So the people who managed that one channel, it's a very centralized

106
00:06:53.495 --> 00:06:56.645
system, they selected what would be on.

107
00:06:57.655 --> 00:06:59.215
And that's what you watch.

108
00:06:59.215 --> 00:07:01.165
So there's not really choice.

109
00:07:01.815 --> 00:07:06.655
So we gradually moved

110
00:07:06.655 --> 00:07:10.575
to a different model. So over time we got more channels that we

111
00:07:10.575 --> 00:07:12.975
could pick. Some people watch one channel,

112
00:07:12.975 --> 00:07:16.175
others watch another channel.

113
00:07:16.175 --> 00:07:21.085
So we have a number of people who decide what is going to be on TV.

114
00:07:21.495 --> 00:07:25.015
And eventually, maybe even moved into a model where we have

115
00:07:25.015 --> 00:07:28.375
streaming services like Netflix, where we can actually decide

116
00:07:28.375 --> 00:07:30.255
ourselves what we're going to watch.

117
00:07:30.255 --> 00:07:34.565
And Netflix still decides what they put on their platform,

118
00:07:35.015 --> 00:07:37.415
but we have a lot more to choose from.

119
00:07:37.415 --> 00:07:42.215
So there is more power to the user of the technology to

120
00:07:42.215 --> 00:07:44.925
decide what you're going to use and what not.

121
00:07:45.855 --> 00:07:49.295
And now we are actually, because of the challenges of today,

122
00:07:49.295 --> 00:07:52.895
why do we have these technology transfer

123
00:07:52.895 --> 00:07:57.175
communities? That's because the challenges that we are facing today

124
00:07:57.175 --> 00:08:01.295
are such that we need different knowledge exchange models.

125
00:08:01.295 --> 00:08:03.135
The old ones don't work anymore.

126
00:08:03.135 --> 00:08:06.205
There are fundamental uncertainties in, for example,

127
00:08:06.975 --> 00:08:10.017
the energy transition, climate adaptation,

128
00:08:10.017 --> 00:08:13.817
the digitalization that is taking place. The inherent

129
00:08:13.817 --> 00:08:17.697
uncertainties in these challenges are such that you can no longer

130
00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:21.297
rely on a centralized or decentralized model where only a few

131
00:08:21.297 --> 00:08:23.567
parties decide what is going to happen.

132
00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:28.697
So instead, we depend on the knowledge that could come from

133
00:08:28.697 --> 00:08:33.097
anywhere. And so we are moving towards this distributed model,

134
00:08:33.097 --> 00:08:36.337
and this is YouTube. In the analogy of the video

135
00:08:36.337 --> 00:08:40.927
entertainment, YouTube is a model where everyone is a broadcaster.

136
00:08:42.137 --> 00:08:45.537
So it is the ultimate democratization, you could say,

137
00:08:45.537 --> 00:08:49.527
of video entertainment. Everyone can produce something,

138
00:08:50.337 --> 00:08:52.977
put it on the platform, and others can watch.

139
00:08:52.977 --> 00:08:58.137
Yeah? So there is not one single party that decides what is going

140
00:08:58.137 --> 00:09:01.297
to be on. Now everything is on.

141
00:09:01.297 --> 00:09:06.297
It is just that you are going to consume part of that platform on the

142
00:09:06.297 --> 00:09:09.177
basis of your preferences, what is suitable to you.

143
00:09:09.177 --> 00:09:13.257
Yeah? Well, the algorithm, of course, decides that for you quite

144
00:09:13.257 --> 00:09:20.127
a bit. So that means for the people who use the technology

145
00:09:20.897 --> 00:09:23.607
is that they have to be aware of how this is changing.

146
00:09:24.617 --> 00:09:28.577
Previously, you could just sit back and wait for what was coming

147
00:09:28.577 --> 00:09:32.697
to you. Now you have to actively look for relevant technologies

148
00:09:32.697 --> 00:09:35.287
yourself, relevant knowledge, where to get it from.

149
00:09:35.897 --> 00:09:38.807
So you have to understand the algorithm,

150
00:09:39.617 --> 00:09:43.727
because the people who send, the broadcasters,

151
00:09:44.377 --> 00:09:45.967
they do not know what you need.

152
00:09:47.097 --> 00:09:49.857
Yeah? They own knowledge about the technology,

153
00:09:49.857 --> 00:09:52.297
but they don't know that it's suitable for you.

154
00:09:52.297 --> 00:09:54.697
And you may not even know what knowledge is around.

155
00:09:54.697 --> 00:09:56.257
So you don't know where to look.

156
00:09:56.257 --> 00:10:01.657
Yeah? So we move in a direction where the

157
00:10:01.657 --> 00:10:03.847
knowledge is around somewhere.

158
00:10:05.057 --> 00:10:07.847
The thing that we need to do is to develop an antenna

159
00:10:08.857 --> 00:10:14.178
that we can use to pick up all the signals that are relevant to us.

160
00:10:14.178 --> 00:10:17.818
So, like in the old days with a radio, where you, you know,

161
00:10:17.818 --> 00:10:23.138
turn the button and then tune to a channel that you like.

162
00:10:23.138 --> 00:10:24.338
Yeah, so you hear some music.

163
00:10:24.338 --> 00:10:26.578
Okay, this is relevant for me.

164
00:10:26.578 --> 00:10:31.458
Yeah. So this is no longer the time to sit back and just

165
00:10:31.458 --> 00:10:32.818
wait for what will come.

166
00:10:32.818 --> 00:10:35.258
Because the people who own the knowledge, they're not going to share

167
00:10:35.258 --> 00:10:37.058
with you. They don't even know that you need it.

168
00:10:37.058 --> 00:10:39.848
Yeah. So it's a completely different way of

169
00:10:40.858 --> 00:10:43.968
how we should manage ourselves in this process.

170
00:10:44.658 --> 00:10:46.408
So this is just a list of

171
00:10:47.658 --> 00:10:51.738
what such a technology exchange community would look like.

172
00:10:51.738 --> 00:10:54.738
And again, for those of you who were here this morning,

173
00:10:54.738 --> 00:10:58.538
I want to again make a case for robustness rather than

174
00:10:58.538 --> 00:11:02.778
resilience. A robust system is one that is very redundant and

175
00:11:02.778 --> 00:11:06.458
heterogeneous, meaning that there is a lot of different types of

176
00:11:06.458 --> 00:11:09.298
knowledge going around that we can pick from.

177
00:11:09.298 --> 00:11:15.168
Because if it's all too like-minded, there is basically no

178
00:11:15.738 --> 00:11:17.618
knowledge in that.

179
00:11:17.618 --> 00:11:23.618
No richness in that. Also, these distributed networks are

180
00:11:23.618 --> 00:11:26.258
typically far more adaptable and scalable.

181
00:11:26.258 --> 00:11:33.218
And maybe as a final message, cooperation is very important

182
00:11:33.218 --> 00:11:39.368
in this. Competition typically leads to a reduction of freedom,

183
00:11:40.138 --> 00:11:44.818
because there is only space left that is left for you by your

184
00:11:44.818 --> 00:11:48.168
competitors. So collaboration typically works a lot better,

185
00:11:48.938 --> 00:11:52.448
because there are no constraints by patents or whatever.

186
00:11:53.898 --> 00:11:58.728
So let me then finish with, sort of, a quote.

187
00:11:59.178 --> 00:12:02.658
Instead of asking ourselves the question, how do we extract the

188
00:12:02.658 --> 00:12:05.978
maximum value from the technology as quickly as possible,

189
00:12:05.978 --> 00:12:08.858
we have to build such a community that I was just talking about.

190
00:12:08.858 --> 00:12:12.859
So a robust tech development model says, how do we ensure technology

191
00:12:12.859 --> 00:12:17.659
successfully adapts, survives, remains durably viable across

192
00:12:17.659 --> 00:12:18.979
unpredictable deviations?

193
00:12:18.979 --> 00:12:23.459
Because, you know, that's what basically a distributed network can

194
00:12:23.459 --> 00:12:26.489
handle much better than what we did in the past.

195
00:12:27.699 --> 00:12:30.139
So I hope that sufficiently resonates with you.

196
00:12:30.139 --> 00:12:32.939
I'm aware that it's a bit abstract, but if you have any

197
00:12:32.939 --> 00:12:35.169
questions, I'm happy to answer them later.
